1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,609
In today's class I'm going to talk about marine archaeology,

2
00:00:06,609 --> 00:00:14,320
the branch of archaeology focusing on human interaction with the sea, lakes and rivers.

3
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:22,260
It's the study of ships, cargoes, shipping facilities, and other physical remains.

4
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:24,338
I'll give you an example,

5
00:00:24,338 --> 00:00:32,650
then go on to show how this type of research is being transformed by the use of the latest technology.

6
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:38,420
Atlit-Yam was a village on the coast of the eastern Mediterranean,

7
00:00:38,420 --> 00:00:44,190
which seems to have been thriving until around 7,000 BC.

8
00:00:44,190 --> 00:00:49,820
The residents kept cattle, caught fish and stored grain

9
00:00:50,130 --> 00:00:52,264
They had wells for fresh water,

10
00:00:52,264 --> 00:00:59,800
many of their houses were built around a courtyard and were constructed of stone.

11
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:03,350
The village contained an impressive monument:

12
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:09,267
seven half-tonne stones standing in a semicircle around a spring,

13
00:01:09,267 --> 00:01:14,000
that might have been used for ceremonial purposes.

14
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,843
Atlit-Yam may have been destroyed swiftly by a tsunami,

15
00:01:18,843 --> 00:01:23,420
or climate change may have caused glaciers to melt

16
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:28,530
and sea levels to rise, flooding the village gradually.

17
00:01:28,530 --> 00:01:34,739
Whatever the cause, it now lies ten metres below the surface of the Mediterranean,

18
00:01:34,739 --> 00:01:38,730
buried under sand at the bottom of the sea.

19
00:01:38,730 --> 00:01:41,700
It's been described as the largest

20
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:47,800
and best preserved prehistoric settlement ever found on the seabed.

21
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:53,150
For marine archaeologists, Atlit-Yam is a treasure trove.

22
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:56,200
Research on the buildings, tools

23
00:01:56,200 --> 00:02:01,323
and the human remains has revealed how the bustling village once functioned,

24
00:02:01,323 --> 00:02:06,130
and even what diseases some of its residents suffered from.

25
00:02:06,130 --> 00:02:14,100
But of course this is only one small village, one window into a lost world.

26
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:19,120
For a fuller picture, researchers need more sunken settlements

27
00:02:19,880 --> 00:02:22,960
but the hard part is finding them.

28
00:02:22,960 --> 00:02:27,929
Underwater research used to require divers to find shipwrecks or artefacts,

29
00:02:27,929 --> 00:02:31,995
but in the second half of the twentieth century,

30
00:02:31,995 --> 00:02:35,740
various types of underwater vehicles were developed

31
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:38,772
some controlled from a ship on the surface,

32
00:02:38,772 --> 00:02:46,000
and some of them autonomous, which means they don't need to be operated by a person.

33
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,481
Autonomous underwater vehicles, or AUVs, are used in the oil industry,

34
00:02:52,481 --> 00:03:00,730
for instance, to create maps of the seabed before rigs and pipelines are installed.

35
00:03:00,730 --> 00:03:05,803
To navigate they use sensors such as compasses and sonar.

36
00:03:05,803 --> 00:03:09,354
Until relatively recently they were very expensive,

37
00:03:09,354 --> 00:03:17,470
and so heavy that they had to be launched from a large vessel with a winch.

38
00:03:17,470 --> 00:03:22,180
But the latest AUVs are much easier to manoeuvre

39
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:27,270
they can be launched from the shore or a small ship.

40
00:03:27,270 --> 00:03:33,150
And they're much cheaper, which makes them more accessible to research teams.

41
00:03:33,150 --> 00:03:39,042
They're also very sophisticated. They can communicate with each other and,

42
00:03:39,042 --> 00:03:44,800
for example, work out the most efficient way to survey a site

43
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:49,220
or to find particular objects on the seabed.

44
00:03:49,220 --> 00:03:52,844
Field tests show the approach can work.

45
00:03:52,844 --> 00:03:56,560
For example, in a trial in 2015

46
00:03:57,040 --> 00:04:02,680
three AUVs searched for wrecks at Marzamemi, off the coast of Sicily.

47
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:07,420
The site is the final resting place of an ancient Roman ship

48
00:04:07,420 --> 00:04:11,788
which sank in the sixth century AD

49
00:04:11,788 --> 00:04:17,540
while ferrying prefabricated marble elements for the construction of an early church.

50
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:21,440
The AUVs mapped the area in detail

51
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:26,000
finding other ships carrying columns of the same material.

52
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,641
Creating an internet in the sea for AUVs to communicate is no easy matter.

53
00:04:33,641 --> 00:04:38,680
Wifi networks on land use electromagnetic waves

54
00:04:39,180 --> 00:04:42,920
but in water these will only travel a few centimetres.

55
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:50,249
Instead, a more complex mix of technologies is required. For short distances,

56
00:04:50,249 --> 00:04:54,600
AUVs can share data using light

57
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,020
while acoustic waves are used to communicate over long distances.

58
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:04,224
But more creative solutions are also being developed,

59
00:05:04,224 --> 00:05:11,057
where an AUV working on the seabed offloads data to a second AUV,

60
00:05:11,057 --> 00:05:18,940
which then surfaces and beams the data home to the research team using a satellite.

61
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:28,060
There's also a system that enables AUVs to share information from seabed scans, and other data.

62
00:05:28,060 --> 00:05:34,800
So if an AUV surveying the seabed finds an intriguing object

63
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:38,022
it can share the coordinates of the object -

64
00:05:38,022 --> 00:05:44,314
that is, its position - with a nearby AUV that carries superior cameras,

65
00:05:44,314 --> 00:05:51,130
and arrange for that AUV to make a closer inspection of the object.

66
00:05:51,130 --> 00:05:58,950
Marine archaeologists are excited about the huge potential of these AUVs for their discipline.

67
00:05:58,950 --> 00:06:06,680
One site where they're going to be deployed is the Gulf of Baratti, off the Italian coast.

68
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:15,860
In 1974, a 2,000-year-old Roman vessel was discovered here, in 18 metres of water.

69
00:06:15,860 --> 00:06:24,040
When it sank, it was carrying medical goods, in wooden or tin receptacles.

70
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:30,349
Its cargo gives us insight into the treatments available all those years ago,

71
00:06:30,349 --> 00:06:38,600
including tablets that are thought to have been dissolved to form a cleansing liquid for the eyes.

72
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:44,700
Other Roman ships went down nearby, taking their cargoes with them.

73
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:48,560
Some held huge pots made of terracotta.

74
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:55,730
Some were used for transporting cargoes of olive oil, and others held wine.

75
00:06:55,730 --> 00:07:00,269
In many cases it's only these containers that remain,

76
00:07:00,269 --> 00:07:06,320
while the wooden ships have been buried under silt on the seabed.

77
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:09,310
Another project that's about to...
